          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is anemia, and does it always mean low iron?",
          "answer": "**What anemia is:** Anemia is a reduced concentration of hemoglobin, the protein in red blood cells that carries oxygen around the body. The condition is described in the *Iron-deficiency anemia* reference.\n\n**Does it always mean low iron?** No. Iron deficiency is the most common cause worldwide, but it is not the only one. The *ICD-10-CM Chapter 3* coding reference says anemia is often a sign of another problem rather than a disease in its own right. It also places anemia of chronic disease and the anemia that accompanies kidney failure in their own category, separate from iron deficiency.\n\nThe passages I retrieved do not describe vitamin B12 or folate deficiency, so I cannot confirm those causes from this reference set. A clinician can explain which causes might apply to a particular situation.\n\n**Why the cause matters:** The iron-deficiency reference notes that iron is lost from the body mainly through bleeding. So low iron in an adult who is not pregnant or menstruating heavily raises the question of where the iron went. This is why a clinician usually looks for the underlying cause rather than only treating the low count.\n\n**When to seek prompt care:** The gastrointestinal bleeding guidance says black, tarry stool can signal bleeding higher in the gut, and that it should not be dismissed. If you notice black stool, vomiting blood, or heavy bleeding, seek medical care promptly. If you have symptoms like these or have questions about your own results, please speak with a clinician.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
